<Project>
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\ProductVersion.props"
          Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\ProductVersion.props')" />
  <!--
    Shared MSBuild properties for every project under modules/.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: modules/ sat outside src/, and src/Directory.Build.props was the repo's
    ONLY one, so no module project inherited any of the shared settings. MSBuild walks UP from the
    project directory to find Directory.Build.props, and modules/AiBrain/ never reaches src/. Each
    module csproj had therefore been repeating (or quietly missing) what src/ sets once.

    The leak this closes, found 2026-09-10 by auditing what actually ships. Every DLL inside the six
    tracked modules-dist/*.zip carried a PE CodeView (RSDS) debug record naming the maintainer's
    absolute build path:

        D:\...\projects\desktop-ai-companion\modules\AiBrain\obj\Release\AiBrain.pdb

    Those zips are served to every user from master via raw.githubusercontent, so that string was
    published. No .pdb is shipped in the zip at all, the payload is the DLL, deps.json and the
    WinRT/SDK assemblies, so the record pointed at a file no user could ever have, and existed
    purely as machine metadata. src/DesktopAICompanion_Portable.csproj already sets DebugType=none
    for Release for the app; this makes the modules match, which is why the app was never affected.

    Deterministic + ContinuousIntegrationBuild alone do NOT fix it: ContinuousIntegrationBuild is
    gated on $(CI) in src/Directory.Build.props, and the module zips are produced LOCALLY by
    packaging/New-ModuleDistZip.ps1 where CI is unset. Suppressing the record is the fix that does
    not depend on who runs the build.

    Debug builds keep full symbols; only Release drops them, and only Release is ever published.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>7.3</LangVersion>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
    <!--
      Warnings are errors here, as in src/Directory.Build.props, and for the same reason as everything
      above: the gap existed because modules/ never reached src/, not because anyone decided modules
      should build to a laxer standard. Added 2026-09-17 after a forced full recompile of the then
      eight module projects came back 0 warnings, so it only bites on a NEW warning.

      No WarningLevel here, on purpose (N-bd-w4-contracts-1-01), which is src's rule too. For a
      .NETCoreApp target the SDK sets it to the target framework's major version
      (Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Analyzers.targets), so every module project, on net10.0-windows or
      net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0, builds at 10 and the compiler's warning waves 5 to 10 are on. This
      file used to pin 4, the .NET Framework value, beside the line below, and a comment here called the
      two "the same two as src". They were not: src had already dropped its pin (N-audit-1106-136), and
      the pin here switched every wave off for all nine module projects, so the line below could never
      turn one of those warnings into an error. Measured 2026-11-14 before removing it: a forced full
      rebuild (no-incremental, Release then Debug) of all nine module projects at the SDK default came
      back 0 warnings each, and a type with an all-lowercase name added to BlinkingLed as a control
      failed its build with CS8981 (wave 7) at the default and built clean under the pin.
      tests/runtime-hardening-selftest.ps1 refuses a pin below the target framework's major version in
      this file or in any project one folder below modules/.

      What it does NOT buy, measured rather than assumed: a write-only private field assigned from
      a non-constant does not raise CS0414 anywhere in this repo, src/ included, where warnings
      are already errors. An earlier note in BACKLOG.md credited that silence to this file's
      missing TreatWarningsAsErrors. That was wrong; the compiler simply does not report it.
    -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">
    <!-- No PDB, and therefore no absolute path embedded in the assembly. -->
    <DebugType>none</DebugType>
    <DebugSymbols>false</DebugSymbols>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
